module Lesson79AuthenticatingProxy exposing [authorizeInternal]

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Lesson 79 — Authenticating proxy: trust a request only when it came through
#             your reverse proxy
#
# lesson: track=stdlib order=810 needs=lesson78-sso
# summary: Turn a reverse-proxy binding header into a ProxyBound proof the compiler demands, so a request that never passed through your proxy is a compile error rather than a runtime gap
#
# Lesson 78 put the whole SSO flow behind one server clause. This lesson is the
# next production question: your app runs BEHIND a reverse proxy (the documented
# nginx cluster), and some internal routes should be reachable ONLY through it.
#
#   THERE ARE TWO WAYS TO TRUST THE EDGE, AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME.
#
#   1. TOPOLOGY.  `listenAddress Loopback` binds 127.0.0.1, so the app is never
#      directly reachable — every request provably arrives through the proxy.
#      That is a claim about the NETWORK, enforced at boot.
#
#   2. A VERIFIED BINDING.  The proxy sets a binding header equal to a shared
#      secret; the app verifies it.  That is a claim about a VALUE, and it holds
#      on any interface — nobody without the secret can forge it.
#
# `Proxy.verifyBinding` is the second one, made unforgeable. It constant-time
# compares the presented binding against the app's configured `Secret` and mints
# a `ProxyBound` fact ONLY on a match. Because no hand-written function may
# declare `ProxyBound` (it is owned by Tesl.Proxy, minted only by this check), a
# function that DEMANDS `ProxyBound` can be reached only by a value that was
# actually verified.
#
#   THE #50.2 DISCRIMINATOR.  A header that IS the assertion (`X-Auth-User: alice`)
#   is only as trustworthy as the topology — it needs `listenAddress Loopback`.
#   A header VERIFIED against stored material (this lesson) needs NO topology
#   claim: it is the same posture as a session cookie. Prefer this form.

import Tesl.Prelude exposing [String]
import Tesl.Crypto exposing [Secret]
import Tesl.Proxy exposing [ProxyBound, Proxy.verifyBinding]

# `internalOnly` DEMANDS `ProxyBound` on its argument. There is no way to call it
# with a binding that was not verified — a request that skipped the proxy does
# not typecheck, so the gap is closed at COMPILE time, not with a runtime 403.
fn internalOnly(bound: String ::: ProxyBound bound) -> String =
  "internal data for a proxy-bound request: " ++ bound

# The edge check. `proxySecret` is the app's configured shared secret; the proxy
# puts the same value in the binding header, which arrives as `presentedBinding`.
# `check` runs the verification and, on success, yields a value carrying
# `ProxyBound` — exactly what `internalOnly` requires.
fn authorizeInternal(proxySecret: Secret, presentedBinding: String) -> String =
  let bound = check Proxy.verifyBinding proxySecret presentedBinding
  internalOnly bound
